{"batchcomplete":true,"query":{"pages":[{"pageid":102306,"ns":0,"title":"Foreplay","revisions":[{"revid":1378031328,"parentid":1376800973,"minor":false,"timestamp":"2026-10-02T15:11:34Z","size":16021,"sha1":"b74ea57d6427db20ff5721fc1bb9339d8059a86d","slots":{"main":{"contentmodel":"wikitext","contentformat":"text/x-wiki","content":"{{Short description|Intimate acts that create sexual arousal}}\n{{about|the sexual activity|the jazz quartet|Fourplay|the EP by [[Saint Motel]]|ForPlay}}\n{{Use dmy dates|date=August 2024}}\n[[File:Martin van Maele - Francion 15.jpg|thumb|[[Martin van Maële]]'s print ''Francion 15'' depicts a couple engaging in foreplay outdoors]]\n'''Foreplay''' is a set of \n[[emotionally intimate|emotionally]] and [[physical intimacy|physically intimate]] acts between one or more people meant to create [[sexual arousal]] and [[sexual desire|desire]] for [[sexual activity]].<ref name=\"Lehmiller\">{{Cite book |title=The psychology of human sexuality |last=Lehmiller, Justin J. |isbn=9781119164708 |edition=Second |location=Hoboken, NJ |oclc=992580729 |date=12 October 2017 }}</ref><ref>{{cite book |last1=Bansal |first1=Sai Prashant |last2=Lamba |first2=Deepika |last3=Somani |first3=Aditya |chapter=Sexual Satisfaction: Foreplay |title=Encyclopedia of Sexual Psychology and Behavior |date=2023 |pages=1–4 |doi=10.1007/978-3-031-08956-5_34-1 |isbn=978-3-031-08956-5 }}</ref> Although foreplay is typically understood as physical sexual activity, nonphysical activities, such as mental or verbal acts, may in some contexts be foreplay.<ref name=\"Lehmiller\"/> This is typically the reason why foreplay tends to be an [[Ambiguous terms|ambiguous term]] and means different things to different people. It can consist of various [[sexual practices]] such as [[kiss]]ing, sexual touching, removing clothes, [[oral sex]], [[Non-penetrative sex#Manual sex|manual sex]], sexual games, and [[sexual roleplay]].<ref name=\"Lehmiller\" />\n\nIt serves as a critical phase in sexual encounters that heightens emotional intimacy and physical pleasure, making the sexual experience more fulfilling and satisfying.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Laan |first1=Ellen T. M. |last2=Klein |first2=Verena |last3=Werner |first3=Marlene A. |last4=van Lunsen |first4=Rik H. W. |last5=Janssen |first5=Erick |date=2 October 2021 |title=In Pursuit of Pleasure: A Biopsychosocial Perspective on Sexual Pleasure and Gender |journal=International Journal of Sexual Health |language=en |volume=33 |issue=4 |pages=516–536 |doi=10.1080/19317611.2021.1965689 |pmid=38595780 |issn=1931-7611 |pmc=10903695 }}</ref> Foreplay has been found to be positively associated with [[female orgasm]].<ref name=\"Shaeer2020\">{{cite journal | author = Shaeer, O.; Skakke, D.; Giraldi, A.; Shaeer, E.; Shaeer, K. | title = Female orgasm and overall sexual function and habits: a descriptive study of a cohort of US women | journal = The Journal of Sexual Medicine | volume = 17 | issue = 6 | pages = 1133–1143 | date = 2020 | doi = 10.1016/j.jsxm.2020.03.008}}</ref>\n\n== Role ==\n\nForeplay has important physical and psychological effects.\n\nPhysically, the sexual organs for both partners receive more [[blood flow]] and become aroused. For male partners, this leads to an [[erection]], and for female partners, this results in a [[clitoral erection]] and [[vaginal lubrication]]. A lack of lubrication may cause [[dyspareunia]]. When partners experience sexual arousal from the acts of foreplay, their organs that are susceptible to pleasure become highly sensitive to external stimuli all while at the same time releasing bodily fluids that lubricate the organs to prepare for intercourse. These physical events aforementioned are more easily reached by the male partner as compared to the female partner which is possibly due to the potential consequences of pregnancy and motherhood.<ref name=\":0\">{{Cite book |last=Fielding |first=Williams J. |title=Sex and The Love Life |publisher=Dodd, Mead & Co. |year=1927 |pages=111–126 |language=English }}</ref> Because of this reason, foreplay has been found to play a critical role for the female partner, as noted by sexologist Dr. William Robinson. Robinson suggests that male partners are able to perform sexual intercourse without the requirement of foreplay, whereas female partners require longer acts of foreplay to become sufficiently stimulated and pleasured.<ref name=\":1\" />\n\nPsychologically, foreplay lowers [[Sexual inhibition|inhibitions]] and increases [[emotional intimacy]] between partners. Both partners, while performing foreplay, are able to have a mutual experience of understanding and emotions. It can also help partners [[sexual communication|communicate their preferences]] more easily, which may help create a more comfortable sexual interaction for both partners.\n\nFrom a biological perspective, foreplay may be seen as an act that is costly in regards to reproduction (male animal perspective). Nonetheless, the costliness of foreplay becomes insufficient when it has been noted through biological research that it is a worthwhile strategy to increase [[fertility]] rates in both the animal and human kingdom. The increased rates of fertility are due to physical and psychological effects aforementioned which also play a role in the effects of the [[neurohypophysial hormone]]. This hormone is capable of increasing the rate production of [[sperm]] count within the male during the acts of extensive foreplay and therefore results with the female partner having higher chances of becoming [[impregnated]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Segura-Sampedro |first=Juan José |date=June 2016 |title=Foreplay, a selected strategy that might be able to increase fertility rates |url=https://linkinghub.elsevier.com/retrieve/pii/S0306987716300226 |journal=Medical Hypotheses |language=en |volume=91 |pages=32–33 |doi=10.1016/j.mehy.2016.04.005 |issn=0306-9877 |oclc=6032721905 |pmid=27142138 |s2cid=13069654 |url-access=subscription }}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Ågmo |first=Anders J. |date=1 November 1975 |title=Neurohypophysial Hormones and the Emission of Semen in Rabbits |journal=Reproduction |language=en |volume=45 |issue=2 |pages=243–248 |doi=10.1530/jrf.0.0450243 |issn=1470-1626 |oclc=4638604223 |pmid=1206623 |s2cid=35155435 |doi-access=free }}</ref>\n\n==Research==\nAccording to a survey on heterosexual couples about duration of sexual intercourse and foreplay, neither partner in these relationships was satisfied with the duration of foreplay in their relationships. This suggests that many couples may experience a gap between what they desire and what typically occurs during sexual activity. This survey sampled 152 couples who were mainly university educated and satisfied with their sexual life. In this study, and when compared to some larger studies, men were better at perceiving desired sexual intercourse and foreplay duration for their partner. The average times spent on intercourse were 7 minutes and 12 minutes on foreplay for the couples in this survey. Another result of this survey was that the length of desired foreplay for men and women was about the same.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Miller |first1=S. Andrea |last2=Byers |first2=Elaine Sandra |author-link2=E. Sandra Byers |date=August 2004 |title=Actual and desired duration of foreplay and intercourse: Discordance and misperceptions within heterosexual couples |url=https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/00224490409552237 |journal=The Journal of Sex Research |volume=41 |issue=3 |pages=301–309 |doi=10.1080/00224490409552237 |issn=0022-4499 |jstor=4423787 |oclc=5546811669 |pmid=15497058 |s2cid=28304924 |url-access=subscription |access-date=17 July 2023 |archive-date=17 July 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230717145248/https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/00224490409552237 |url-status=live }}</ref>\n\nIn a global study of about 12,000 individuals from 27 countries in 6 continents, physical foreplay was rated as \"very important\" for 63% of men and 60% of women.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Mulhall |first1=John |last2=King |first2=Rosie |last3=Glina |first3=Sidney |last4=Hvidsten |first4=Kyle |date=April 2008 |title=Importance of and Satisfaction with Sex Among Men and Women Worldwide: Results of the Global Better Sex Survey |url=https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1743609515319962 |journal=[[The Journal of Sexual Medicine]] |volume=5 |issue=4 |pages=788–795 |doi=10.1111/j.1743-6109.2007.00765.x |issn=1743-6095 |oclc=5154200064 |pmid=18284556 |s2cid=3426861 |url-access=subscription |access-date=17 July 2023 |archive-date=17 July 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230717150754/https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1743609515319962 |url-status=live }}</ref>\n\nAccording to a study of individuals in committed [[Romantic relationship|romantic relationships]], pornography and sexual media usage does not play a role in satisfaction with time spent on foreplay, although other aspects of sexual satisfaction can be impacted by this type of sexual media. This means that foreplay is an important part of the sexual script, and social influences on the sexual script such as pornography and provocative sexual media do not impact foreplay. Time spent on foreplay is an important part of becoming sexually aroused and unique to each individual, and individuals still need the same amount of foreplay in order to become sexually aroused despite misconceptions surrounding what people learn from consuming pornography.<ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Leonhardt |first1=Nathan D. |last2=Willoughby |first2=Brian J. |date=February 2019 |title=Pornography, provocative sexual media, and their differing associations with multiple aspects of sexual satisfaction |url=https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0265407517739162 |journal=Journal of Social and Personal Relationships |volume=36 |issue=2 |pages=618–641 |doi=10.1177/0265407517739162 |issn=0265-4075 |s2cid=149214569 |url-access=subscription |access-date=17 July 2023 |archive-date=17 July 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230717145248/https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/0265407517739162 |url-status=live }}</ref>\n\n== Games ==\n\n[[file:nipping the Nape.png|thumb|Foreplay by kissing a woman's back moving towards her hips]]{{Unreferenced|section|date=October 2026}}\n[[Sexual roleplay|Sexual role-playing]] or sex games can create sexual interest. These games can be played in a variety of situations, and have been enhanced by technology. This type of extended foreplay can involve SMS messaging ([[sexting]]), phone calls, online chat, or other forms of distance communication, which are intended to stimulate fantasizing about the forthcoming encounter. This form of foreplay does not require physical presence, as the tantalization builds sexual tension.\n\nA card or board game can be played for foreplay. The objective of the game is for the partners to indulge their fantasies. The loser can, for example, be required to [[strip game|remove clothing]] or give the winner a sensual [[foot massage]] or any other thing that the winner wants to try. A sensuous atmosphere can also be enhanced by candles, drinks, sensual food or suggestive clothing. Even a suggestion of the use of [[sex toys]] or the playing of games involving [[Sexual fetishism|fetish]], [[sexual bondage]], or blindfolding is an indication of [[sexual interest]].\n\nSome couples create sexual interest by watching erotic and [[pornography|pornographic]] videos. [[Role play]]ing may involve the partners wearing costumes, to create and maintain a [[sexual fantasy]]. For variety, the partners can make up a (sexual) story together. One of them starts with a sentence and then the other continues until the story becomes sexually explicit, and it provides an opportunity for the partners to express their sexual fantasies. \"Strangers for a day\" is a role-playing game which consists of the couple playing roles of the first meeting between them. In a public meeting place, the partners pretend to be strangers meeting for the first time. The objective is for them to flirt and seduce the other, without doing or saying anything that they normally would not do or say at a first meeting.\n\n==Tantric==\nTantric foreplay is the first step in the lovemaking session, according to the [[tantra]] principles. [[Tantric sex]] is against rushing things for the purpose of reaching an [[orgasm]], so tantric foreplay is a way to prepare the body and the mind for the union between the two bodies. The tantric rules say that foreplay must be focused on the preparation before sexual intercourse. Tantric foreplay may include sensual baths between the two partners in a relaxing atmosphere. [[Fragrance oil]] and candles may also be used to set up the mood.<ref>{{cite book |last=Van Lysebeth |first=Andre |title=Tantra: the cult of the feminine |year=2002 |publisher=Motilal Banarsidass Publ. |isbn=978-81-208-1759-3 }}</ref>\n\nTantric foreplay is only about giving each other time to connect [[Spirituality|spiritually]] and bind. Staring at each other while in a cross-legged position and touching the other's hand palms is a usual foreplay tactic used by tantra practitioners.\nTantric foreplay may also include [[Tantra massage]]s. The massage that is applied, according to the tantric philosophy, is not for reaching orgasms but for giving each other pleasure and connecting at a spiritual level.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://mentalforeplay.com/pag24.php |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20111123012018/http://mentalforeplay.com/pag24.php |archive-date=23 November 2011 |title=Tantra foreplay techniques |author=Mental Foreplay online portal |date=18 February 2010 }}</ref>\n\n== History ==\nThere are many historical references to foreplay, with many artistic depictions. The [[History of India|Ancient Indian]] work ''[[Kama Sutra]]'' mentions different types of embracing, kissing, and marking with nails and teeth.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.businessbuildersbanquet.com/software/kamasutra.pdf|title=The Kama Sutra of Vatsayayana: Translation by Sir Richard Burton|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160305175755/http://www.businessbuildersbanquet.com/software/kamasutra.pdf |archive-date=5 March 2016 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|website=Internet Sacred Text Archive|url=http://www.sacred-texts.com/sex/kama/kama205.htm|title=Kama Sutra Chapter V: On Biting, and the Means to be Employed with regard to Women of Different Countries|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211203071615/https://www.sacred-texts.com/sex/kama/kama205.htm |archive-date=3 December 2021 }}</ref><ref>{{cite web|website=Internet Sacred Text Archive|url=http://www.sacred-texts.com/sex/kama/kama204.htm|title=Kama Sutra Chapter IV: On Pressing, or Marking, or Scratching with the Nails|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210507112053/https://www.sacred-texts.com/sex/kama/kama204.htm |archive-date=7 May 2021 }}</ref> It also mentions [[BDSM]] activities such as slapping and moaning as \"play\".<ref>{{cite web|website=Internet Sacred Text Archive|url=http://www.sacred-texts.com/sex/kama/kama207.htm|title=Kama Sutra Chapter VII: Of the Various Modes of Striking, and of the Sounds Appropriate to them|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071013025946/http://www.sacred-texts.com/sex/kama/kama207.htm |archive-date=13 October 2007 }}</ref> There are also examples of the use of foreplay in marital advice literature that dates back to the early 1900s.<ref name=\":1\">{{Cite journal |last=Laipson |first=Peter |date=1 March 1996 |title=\"Kiss Without Shame, For She Desires It\": Sexual Foreplay in American Marital Advice Literature, 1900-1925 |url=https://academic.oup.com/jsh/article-abstract/29/3/507/976272?redirectedFrom=fulltext |journal=Journal of Social History |volume=29 |issue=3 |pages=507–525 |doi=10.1353/jsh/29.3.507 |issn=0022-4529 |url-access=sub